"reference.tableTitle" = "TOUR BASELINE";
"reference.sourceTitle" = "WHERE THESE NUMBERS COME FROM";
"reference.sourceText" = "Every figure in Puttor is measured against the PGA Tour, and that yardstick is Mark Broadie's work at Columbia University. His putts-gained paper is built on ShotLink, the tour's tracking system, which records the position of every putt to within an inch — millions of putts, not a sample. From that he publishes how many putts a tour player needs on average from each distance: 1.61 from 10 feet, exactly 2.0 from 33 feet. Those figures and the tour's published make rates are what the two curves above were fitted to, and Puttor stays within about a hundredth of a stroke of them. Strokes gained follows his definition unchanged: the putts the tour would have needed from where you stood, less the putts you actually took. PCG is not his — it is Puttor's own reading of the same make rates, scoring one stroke against the odds of holing it.";
"reference.sourceLink" = "Broadie: Putts Gained — Measuring Putting on the PGA TOUR (PDF)";
"reference.curvesTitle" = "THE CURVES BEHIND THE TABLE";
"reference.curvesText" = "Both columns are read off fitted curves, with x = ln(distance in metres), from 0.3 m to 40 m.";
